// Advanced sequence: Level 11 onward. Every concept has a specific example, a why,
// and an observation. Route labels are illustrative, not universal team terminology.
const C=(name,detail,example,insight,watch)=>({name,detail,example,insight,watch});
const Q=(stem,choices,right,explanation)=>[stem,choices,right,explanation];
const L=(number,title,summary,goals,concepts,quiz)=>({id:`core-${number}`,number,title,category:number<=13?'Scheme foundations':number<=17?'Read and adjust':'College game intelligence',duration:22,summary,goals,concepts:concepts.map(c=>[c.name,c.detail,c.example]),coaching:concepts.map(c=>[c.insight,c.watch]),quiz});

export const collegiateLessons=[
L(11,'Fronts, Gaps & Defensive Structure','Build a defensive map before learning the calls: who owns each gap, who sets the edge, and who supports the pass.',
['Identify gap and technique labels','Explain the force/fit/alley relationship','Distinguish pre-snap shell from actual coverage'],[
C('Gap language and techniques','An A gap is between center and guard, B between guard and tackle, and C outside the tackle. Defensive technique numbers describe where a defender aligns relative to an offensive lineman; techniques and gaps are related but not identical.','A shade nose on the center may attack an A gap, while a 3-technique on the guard may be assigned to penetrate a B gap.','This vocabulary lets you read an entire front instead of saying “the defenders are close to the line.” The defense wants every likely running lane handled without leaving the pass uncovered.','Identify the center, both guards, and both tackles. Trace A, B and C gaps before naming any technique.'),
C('Box count and run math','The box is the area near the ball containing front defenders and frequently linebackers. An offense can compare blockers with box defenders, but location and assignment matter more than a raw number. A safety may join the fit after the snap.','Six blockers against a six-man box looks balanced, but a late-rotating safety can create an extra run defender.','Defenses disguise who is actually involved in the run fit. An apparently light box can become heavy after the ball is snapped, so good offenses confirm the picture.','Count immediate box defenders, then note whether a safety inserts into the run fit on the snap.'),
C('Force, spill and alley','Defensive run fits have complementary roles. A force player prevents an outside escape; interior defenders fill or spill runs; an alley defender closes the lane created between the edge and pursuit. Exact rules differ by system.','Against an outside run, a corner may force the runner back in while a safety fills the alley inside the corner.','A defense works as a linked system. Winning an individual block but abandoning force can give the runner a much bigger lane around the edge.','Pause outside runs and identify who sets the widest edge, who fills inside, and who would clean up the ball carrier.'),
C('One-high and two-high are shells','One or two deep safeties form a visible shell, not a guaranteed coverage. Teams can rotate from two-high to middle-of-field-closed coverage or exchange assignments after the snap.','Two deep safeties before the snap can disguise Cover 3 with one safety dropping into underneath support.','A quarterback should use the shell as an opening hypothesis, not the final answer. The post-snap movement of safeties changes where routes are actually vulnerable.','Freeze the pre-snap shell; then compare where both safeties are at the first passing decision.'),
C('Structure before guessing plays','A useful pre-snap report describes personnel, front, box, leverage and safety shell before naming a coverage. This avoids assuming an entire scheme from one moving defender.','“Nickel, four-down front, six in the box, two-high shell” is more useful than “definitely Cover 2.”','Separating visible evidence from interpretation is the first step toward reliable collegiate film analysis. It also makes mistakes diagnosable after the play.','List three directly observable facts before making a coverage or run-fit prediction.')],
[Q('An offensive tackle lines up outside a guard. Which gap is between those two?', ['A gap','B gap','C gap'],1,'The B gap is between the guard and tackle.'),Q('Why can a six-man box become a seven-man fit after the snap?',['A safety inserts into the run fit','The offense gains a twelfth blocker','The line of scrimmage moves'],0,'A safety rotating down may add a late defender.'),Q('What is the force defender trying to prevent?', ['Outside escape by the runner','A legal forward pass anywhere','A center snap'],0,'Force keeps the runner from easily getting outside.'),Q('Two deep safeties before the snap guarantee what?', ['A two-high final coverage','Only a starting shell','No blitz'],1,'Safeties can rotate to different post-snap assignments.'),Q('A 3-technique normally lines up in relation to what?', ['The outside shoulder of a guard','The center of the end zone','The wide receiver'],0,'Technique numbers identify defensive alignment on linemen.'),Q('A corner contains the edge and a safety fills the inside lane. Which combination is illustrated?',['Force and alley support','Two eligible forward passes','Pass-protection slide'],0,'The corner sets force and the safety fills the alley.'),Q('What is the best initial coverage report?', ['“Definitely Cover 2” from one safety','“Two-high shell, check rotation after snap”','“The front tells every route”'],1,'Describe the visible shell before confirming the post-snap coverage.'),Q('Why is counting blockers versus box defenders insufficient by itself?', ['Technique, movement and run assignments change the math','Numbers are banned in football','Box players cannot defend the run'],0,'Alignment, gaps and late run fits matter beyond the count.'),Q('Which defender is most clearly responsible for the C gap?', ['A defender assigned outside the tackle','A defender assigned between center and guard','The center who snaps'],0,'The C gap is immediately outside the tackle, but actual assignments vary.'),Q('Which observation most strongly tests your pre-snap prediction?', ['Post-snap safety rotation','Uniform color','Who won the coin toss'],0,'Safeties moving at the snap can reveal a different coverage picture.')]),
L(12,'Run Game Families: Zone, Duo & Gap','Study the blocking rules and defender reads that distinguish college run families.',
['Differentiate zone and gap blocking','Explain the runner’s landmark and read','Predict defensive counters'],[
C('Inside zone rules','Inside zone uses coordinated blocking toward a defined direction and double-team-to-linebacker combinations as available. The runner reads how the front moves and can press the intended path before making one decisive cut.','Against a shaded nose and a 3-technique, blockers may combination-block the down lineman and work to the near linebacker.','A zone scheme is not simply “run through the middle.” Linemen follow shared rules; the runner reads leverage to choose a crease that actually appears.','Identify the initial play direction, paired blockers, and which linebacker could receive a climber.'),
C('Outside zone and the edge','Outside zone threatens the perimeter. The line works laterally to create reach or run-off opportunities, while the runner presses an outside track and may plant to cut upfield or back.','If the edge defender widens to stop the outside track, the runner may find a crease inside that defender.','The defense must move horizontally before tackling north-south. That horizontal displacement creates opportunities for a decisive cutback.','Track how defenders flow laterally, then see whether the back bends or cuts through a new crease.'),
C('Power and counter structure','Power is commonly a gap-scheme run with a down-blocking surface, a puller and an edge or kick-out assignment. Counter commonly uses initial backfield action opposite the final run direction and may feature two pullers.','On power, the backside guard can pull through while the front side blocks down; the back follows the intended crease.','The play structure assigns different jobs to blockers; “a pulling guard” alone does not identify the complete play. Understanding who kicks and who leads explains the running lane.','Pause the backside guard and identify their destination. Watch whether the edge defender is kicked out or logged.'),
C('Duo is not automatically inside zone','Duo is often taught as a downhill, double-team-driven run. It can look similar to inside zone, but blocking rules, linebacker targets and the runner’s read differ by offense.','If the offensive line drives paired defenders vertically and the back reads the front-side linebacker, the play may be duo.','Film study should use blocking behavior and reads, not just the direction the ball carrier moved. Coaches define variations differently, so avoid declaring a scheme from one frame.','Compare offensive line footwork, which linebackers the doubles target, and the back’s initial aiming point.'),
C('Answers to run fits','Defenses change gap fit, spill/box technique, safety support and front alignment to defeat run designs. Offenses use complementary runs, tags or play-action when defenders overcommit.','A hard-setting edge can slow outside zone, while an overaggressive downhill safety may expose play-action space behind him.','Run and pass calls are connected: run fits create passing windows and defensive pressure can make a run look good before the snap but poor after the snap.','After each run, name the defender who forced the ball carrier to change direction and which gap was left vulnerable.')],
[Q('A run with coordinated lateral displacement toward the edge is most associated with?', ['Outside zone','Punt protection','Victory formation'],0,'Outside zone threatens the perimeter and stretches defenders laterally.'),Q('What should an inside-zone runner read?', ['Available creases based on defensive leverage','Only the scoreboard','The nearest sideline marker'],0,'Inside zone runner decisions depend on actual block movement.'),Q('Which run commonly uses a pulling guard with down blocks?', ['Power','Inside zone always','Four verticals'],0,'Power often includes a backside puller and down blocks.'),Q('Why does counter action matter?', ['It can influence defenders away from the actual run','It makes a pass automatic','It guarantees no defender can tackle'],0,'Backfield misdirection affects defenders’ initial reads.'),Q('What can distinguish duo from inside zone?', ['Blocking rules and runner keys','Whether the ball is brown','The number of game quarters'],0,'Similar-looking runs can have different line assignments and runner reads.'),Q('A force player widens with outside zone. What might the offense gain?', ['An inside cutback crease','An extra down','A legal twelfth offensive player'],0,'Widening a force player can create an inside lane.'),Q('When film shows a puller, what should be analyzed next?', ['The puller’s block target and following lead path','Assume it is definitely counter','Ignore other blockers'],0,'The target of the pull and nearby blocking rules identify play structure.'),Q('Which defender can defeat a run despite equal initial box numbers?', ['A safety entering the fit after the snap','A receiver on the sideline','A player in the substitution area'],0,'Post-snap force or alley involvement changes the fit.'),Q('A guard and tackle double a lineman and climb to a linebacker. What is that?', ['A combination block','A forward lateral','A corner route'],0,'A combination block works a down defender and climbs to the next level.'),Q('Why pair play-action with a successful run scheme?', ['To influence run-keying defenders and open passing windows','To pause the play clock permanently','To add blockers after the snap'],0,'Play action can exploit defensive reactions to a convincing run presentation.')]),
L(13,'Passing Concepts & Progressions','Move from route names to full-field structures, defender conflicts and actual quarterback progressions.',
['Identify horizontal and vertical stretches','Read a concept, not a single route','Account for rush timing and outlets'],[
C('Routes are components, concepts are systems','A curl, slant or go route has limited meaning alone. Spacing, Stick, Smash, Mesh and Flood coordinate multiple routes to stress specific defenders and create progressions for the passer.','On Smash, the outside receiver may run a short hitch while the inside receiver runs a corner, creating a high-low stress on a corner or flat defender.','A complete passing concept defines eligible-player assignments, quarterback footwork, intended progression, protection and an answer when the defense removes the first option.','For every diagram, describe how two routes work together and which defender is caught between them.'),
C('Horizontal stretches: spacing and stick','Spacing sends receivers into short zones across the width of the field. Stick commonly pairs a quick stick/settle route with a flat threat, making the apex defender choose width or depth.','If the apex flies out to the flat, the stick route may settle in the vacated short window; if apex sits inside, the flat can open.','Horizontal stretches make the quarterback read defender leverage rather than selecting the first receiver who appears open in a frozen image.','Track the apex defender, then compare what becomes available on their inside and outside.'),
C('Vertical stretches: flood and sail','A Flood or Sail family places receivers at three distinct depths to one side: a deep clearing route, intermediate out-breaking route and short flat outlet. Against some three-deep zones this can overload an outside coverage lane.','In Trips Right Flood, No. 1 clears vertically, No. 2 runs a 12–15-yard out, and No. 3 releases toward the flat; assignments and depths vary by playbook.','The quarterback reads the outside coverage structure high-to-low or by a team-defined progression. A deep route can create room even when it is never targeted.','Watch whether the flat defender sinks under the intermediate route or drives down toward the outlet.'),
C('Mesh and man-beating traffic','Mesh usually uses two shallow crossers from opposite sides and an additional sit, corner, or wheel element. It can exploit man coverage through traffic and also find sit-down space versus zone when taught correctly.','Two shallow receivers cross near the middle; against man, defenders must navigate the crossing paths without losing leverage.','Mesh is not simply two receivers running toward one another. Receivers must keep prescribed depth and spacing, and the quarterback must know where the additional outlet lives.','Trace the defenders assigned to shallow crossers and see which coverage rules handle the crossing routes.'),
C('Progressions and protection live together','A quarterback may use a coverage-based progression, a key-defender read or a timed sequence. The protection determines whether a slow-developing concept is realistic against pressure.','A five-man protection against a known sixth rusher may require an immediate built-in hot answer or a protection adjustment.','Good play structure connects the route timing and the rush count. A theoretically open deep route is not useful if the quarterback must throw before it breaks.','Start a clock at the snap. Which option is open before the rush reaches the quarterback?')],
[Q('Smash is commonly built around which two-route pairing?', ['Hitch and corner','Two backward laterals','Two punt coverages'],0,'The hitch and corner can create a high-low read on a corner or flat defender.'),Q('Which player is the key read on a basic Stick/flat pairing?', ['The apex defender','The referee','The center after the snap'],0,'The apex must manage inside stick and outside flat threats.'),Q('Which three-depth structure best describes Flood?', ['Vertical clear, intermediate out, flat outlet','Three identical shallow slants','Three handoffs'],0,'Flood stresses a side of the coverage vertically.'),Q('What does Mesh primarily involve?', ['Crossing shallow routes','Two defensive line stunts','Back-to-back punts'],0,'Mesh uses paired crossers with complementary options.'),Q('Why does a non-targeted go route matter?', ['It can clear deep coverage for an underneath route','Only targets affect defenders','It automatically scores'],0,'Clearing routes reshape defenders’ space.'),Q('The flat defender sinks under an intermediate out. What might open in a Flood read?', ['The short flat outlet','The referee’s sideline','The center’s pass rush'],0,'If the defender sinks, the flat often becomes available.'),Q('Which makes a pass concept complete?', ['Routes, read, protection, timing and outlet','Only one arrow for the primary receiver','Only the formation name'],0,'A complete call accounts for everyone and the quarterback’s decision process.'),Q('What is a key-defender read?', ['A decision based on how one responsible defender reacts','Reading the quarterback’s jersey number','A universal fixed first target'],0,'Many concepts deliberately put one defender into a conflict.'),Q('Why might a concept fail against a six-man rush with five protectors?', ['The QB can be pressured before routes develop','The ball is illegal on third down','The coverage automatically switches teams'],0,'Protection and release timing constrain the pass.'),Q('A defender follows both shallow crossers with different teammates trading them. What might that suggest?', ['A coverage exchange or match rule','A forced offsides penalty','An automatic touchdown'],0,'Defenders may pass routes through zones or use match rules rather than pure man trail.')]),
L(14,'Coverage Families & Match Rules','Learn not only where defenders stand, but how route distributions determine their real assignments.',
['Compare Cover 1, 2, 3 and quarters','Differentiate spot-drop from pattern match','Understand route distribution and safety help'],[
C('Cover 1: man with a post safety','Cover 1 commonly uses man coverage underneath with a deep middle safety. A robber or low-hole defender may help underneath. Corner leverage depends on where help is located.','With a single post safety, a corner may use inside or outside leverage according to the call, route threat and help.','Calling “Cover 1” does not give every defender the same coaching rule. Identify the post safety, coverage matchups and whether low-hole help is available.','Identify who has vertical responsibility for each receiver and where the free safety can assist.'),
C('Cover 2: split deep halves','Traditional Cover 2 assigns two deep-half players and generally five underneath zone responsibilities. The corner can be a cloud/flat defender depending on the coverage variation. Hole shots and seams require precise timing and can be bracketed by underneath defenders.','A receiver stretching between a sinking corner in the flat and a deep-half safety can create a tight intermediate window.','The number “2” does not mean two cornerbacks. Learn the landmark rules, depth of the safeties and which underneath defenders support them.','Watch the corner’s depth and whether the safety squeezes a vertical route near the sideline.'),
C('Cover 3: three deep lanes','Cover 3 typically assigns three deep thirds and four underneath zones in a basic seven-man coverage. It can use match rules, rotation and simulated pressure that change the picture.','In a common Sky rotation, one safety drops to the curl/flat area as the other middle safety occupies the deep middle third.','The outside thirds and middle third are structural responsibilities, not guarantees about who will occupy them; teams rotate and exchange defenders.','Freeze the first three steps after the snap and count the final deep defenders rather than pre-snap safeties.'),
C('Quarters and route distribution','Quarters can be spot-drop or pattern-match. Modern match quarters often asks a safety to respond to No. 2 vertical differently than No. 2 immediately out or under. Teams have distinct “MEG”, “MOD”, “Palms” and other rule families.','When No. 2 releases vertically, the safety may match No. 2; on a fast No. 2 out, some quarter-quarter-half or Palms-style calls switch responsibilities.','Simply drawing four deep zones would conceal the actual challenge. Quarters requires decisions based on receiver releases, which makes it difficult to teach from a static picture alone.','Identify No. 1 and No. 2 to each side, then follow the safety when No. 2 goes vertically versus to the flat.'),
C('When the shell lies','A defense can show two high and rotate to Cover 3, show single high and bail to two deep, or disguise a pressure. A quarterback must update the read at the snap and protect against late movement.','Pre-snap two-high becomes one-high when the field safety rotates to center field and the boundary safety comes down to curl/flat.','A useful film explanation states the initial prediction, new evidence at the snap, and the revised answer. Real high-level reads are conditional rather than telepathic.','Compare the safeties’ pre-snap locations with their responsibilities when the quarterback sets to throw.')],
[Q('What best summarizes Cover 1?', ['Man underneath, usually a deep middle safety','Four deep spot-drop zones','Zero defensive backs'],0,'Cover 1 often pairs man coverage with middle-of-field help.'),Q('Traditional Cover 2 commonly uses how many deep halves?', ['Two','Three','Four'],0,'Two players defend the deep halves in a traditional Cover 2 structure.'),Q('A base Cover 3 uses which deep-zone structure?', ['Three deep thirds','Five deep halves','Man on every receiver only'],0,'Cover 3 commonly divides the deep field into thirds.'),Q('Which route can trigger a match-quarters safety to carry No. 2?', ['No. 2 running vertically','No. 2 never releasing','No. 2 taking a knee before snap'],0,'Pattern-match quarters can carry vertical No. 2 based on coverage rules.'),Q('What is meant by No. 2?', ['Second receiver from the sideline on a side','Second quarterback of the half','Backside defensive tackle'],0,'Receiver numbering is often outside-in on each side.'),Q('Why is a two-high shell not definitive?', ['Safeties can rotate after the snap','No coverage has safeties','All two-high shells are Cover 2'],0,'Pre-snap shells disguise final coverage rules.'),Q('A defender exchanges an underneath route to a teammate. What does that suggest?', ['Zone or pattern-match exchange','Every defender is in pure man trail','The referee changed the play'],0,'Defenders may exchange crossers according to match/zone rules.'),Q('What should you check to distinguish quarters from a generic four-deep drawing?', ['Actual reactions to vertical and flat releases','Jersey shades','Whether the quarterback claps'],0,'Pattern-match responsibilities depend on route distribution.'),Q('Against Cover 1, which asset may help underneath crossers?', ['A robber or low-hole defender','An extra end zone','A second center'],0,'Some Cover 1 variants add an underneath helper.'),Q('The pre-snap shell is two high; one safety drops and one stays in the post. What did you learn?', ['The defense rotated to a single-high structure','The offense is offside automatically','It must be Cover 2'],0,'A post-snap single-high picture should update the QB read.')]),
L(15,'Pass Protection & Pressure Packages','Understand protection structure, extra rushers and the quick answers that keep a passing concept alive.',
['Explain six-man rush against five-man protection','Identify common slide principles','Recognize pressure replacements and simulated rush'],[
C('Count available rushers and protectors','The offensive line supplies five blockers, but running backs or tight ends can be involved. Five blockers cannot individually block six immediate rushers without changing the read, release or protection plan.','A six-man pressure against five in protection requires the QB to know the unblocked threat, throw hot, or adjust blocking resources.','Do not label a play “bad blocking” until you know the protection count. A deliberately unblocked player can be an intentional part of the quarterback’s read.','Count defenders who actually rush after the snap and identify anyone intentionally left unblocked.'),
C('Half-slide and full-slide principles','A half-slide scheme often combines zone/area rules on one side with man assignments on the other. Full-slide emphasizes coordinated area protection, with a back commonly responsible for a defined edge or scan. Real rules depend on the offense.','The center calls a slide left, the line works its rules, and a back scans for the remaining threat on the opposite edge.','Protection is an offensive team system, not just five independent fights. The same defensive alignment may require a different blocking call with a different route release.','Find where the center points or where the linemen step, and identify the back’s check-release responsibility.'),
C('Identify the Mike and the pressure picture','Some offenses name a Mike linebacker as an identification point to organize protection and count. The “Mike” in a call is not necessarily the opponent’s actual middle linebacker.','A center points at an outside linebacker as the declared Mike to set the slide and sorting rules against an overloaded front.','Teaching “Mike means the actual middle linebacker” fails as soon as a defense moves. The important element is how the offensive line and back share the protection call.','Watch pre-snap center/QB signals and whether the line responds as a coordinated unit.'),
C('Zone blitz and simulated pressure','A zone blitz can send an unexpected rusher while another apparent rusher drops into coverage. Simulated pressure or a creeper commonly changes which four rush while preserving a four-man rush count, so pressure does not always mean more than four.','A linebacker rushes off the edge as a defensive end drops underneath a slant window: the quarterback must see the replacement defender.','The replacement coverage is often the real trap. A quarterback throwing blindly into a “vacated” lane can hit a dropping defensive end.','Identify not just who comes, but who leaves the line and occupies the throwing window.'),
C('Hot throws, sight adjustments and timing','A hot answer is a quick route or adjustment available when the protection cannot block a threat. It only works if quarterback and receiver share the rule. Changing a route without coordinating protection can make a bad situation worse.','An uncovered edge rush may trigger a quick stick or slant, provided the throwing lane and coverage are confirmed.','A pass play must be taught as formation + protection + routes + progression + pressure answer. The opponent’s rush dictates how much of the progression is available.','After identifying an extra rusher, trace the QB’s earliest realistic passing lane and a likely replacement defender.')],
[Q('Six rushers attack five protectors. What must the offense have?', ['A defined answer for an unblocked threat or protection adjustment','A sixth offensive lineman materializes','No quarterback read'],0,'The count requires a plan, whether quick throw, altered protection or another adjustment.'),Q('What does half-slide combine in many systems?', ['Area rules on one side, man rules on the other','Only special-teams blocks','Two quarterbacks'],0,'Half-slide often mixes zone and man protection principles.'),Q('Why might the QB declare a Mike who is not a traditional middle linebacker?', ['To set the offense’s counting/protection rules','To signal a touchdown','Because center cannot snap'],0,'Mike identification may be a protection reference point.'),Q('Which best describes a creeper/simulated pressure?', ['Changing rush origins, often with four rushers total','Always rushing nine defenders','Never dropping a lineman'],0,'Simulated pressure can change the rush without sacrificing the four-man coverage numbers.'),Q('A defensive end drops into a slant window while a linebacker rushes. Why is that dangerous?', ['The replacement defender can intercept a rushed throw','The offense gets a free down','The linebacker must stop'],0,'Pressure replacements can occupy the vacated passing lane.'),Q('A running back scans for a late rusher then releases. What is that?', ['Check-release protection','Illegal receiver motion automatically','Cover 4'],0,'The back confirms protection before becoming an outlet.'),Q('What determines if a slow deep concept is practical?', ['Protection time versus route timing','Jersey number of receiver','Only the down marker color'],0,'A throw cannot wait longer than protection survives.'),Q('Why is the Mike declaration not a prediction of the play?', ['It organizes blocking counts and rules','It names the defensive coordinator','It forces man coverage'],0,'Offenses use declared landmarks for their assignments.'),Q('Against overload pressure, which may be safer?', ['Slide or adjust and supply a hot answer','Make all receivers run deeper','Ignore the overloaded side'],0,'Overload forces immediate sorting or throwing choices.'),Q('What should film study identify after a blitz?', ['Both rush paths and replacement defenders','Only the sack result','Only the touchdown score'],0,'Replacement coverage reveals where the defense is trying to trap a hot throw.')]),
L(16,'Quarterback Reads: Before and After the Snap','Make a plan from evidence, verify post-snap rotations and connect each read to a route progression.',
['Distinguish pre-snap clues from confirmations','Make a one-defender high-low read','Re-set the answer against late pressure'],[
C('Set a hypothesis pre-snap','A quarterback can count the box, classify a safety shell and note receiver leverage before the snap. None of those clues alone proves the coverage. A disciplined QB enters each call with likely answers and a plan if the defense rotates.','A two-high shell suggests checking deep safety movement after the snap before trusting the anticipated corner route.','Good college reads are “if X, then Y,” not “I guessed Cover 2, so throw the corner.” The QB must respond to what the defenders actually do.','State one likely defensive structure and two post-snap clues that would confirm or disprove it.'),
C('MOFC versus MOFO after the snap','Middle-of-field-closed (MOFC) often describes a single central deep defender; middle-of-field-open (MOFO) often describes split safety coverage. The post-snap read informs which route stresses are attractive.','If safeties split and remain high, a seam read differs from a rotation where one safety claims the post.','MOFC/MOFO are starting classifications; quarters match, trap and bracket details still determine whether a seam or post is truly available.','Pause at QB’s hitch and count actual post defenders, then examine underneath leverage.'),
C('Read the conflict player','Many concepts isolate a defender with two threats. On Stick the apex is stressed by an inside settle and flat release; on Flood the curl/flat defender must balance underneath and intermediate routes.','A curl/flat defender expands toward the flat, and the inside stick window becomes available behind him.','This is an actual football read: defender moves first, quarterback throws second. It improves decision quality compared with memorizing a “best” receiver for each play.','Keep the ball fixed and follow only the designated key defender until the throw decision.'),
C('Progression, leverage and reset','A progression might start with a presnap favorable matchup, then work high-to-low or across the field. If the defense eliminates the first answer, QB eyes and feet reset to the next eligible read according to the protection time.','The corner route is capped by safety help, so the quarterback works down to an underneath route rather than forcing the ball into a bracket.','Progression order and defender key reads can coexist. The quarterback must not confuse “first look” with “throw it regardless of coverage.”','Mark where the QB’s eyes should move when the first receiver is capped or covered.'),
C('Pressure can change the read','Protection issues may shorten the route progression or create a hot answer. A defense can disguise a dropping lineman in the very lane that appears opened by a blitz.','An unblocked linebacker comes; a defensive end replaces him underneath, so the QB must check the hot throwing lane instead of automatically throwing slant.','Good post-snap intelligence integrates protection and coverage. The smartest target is not necessarily the deepest or most open receiver in a static frame.','Count the rush first, then verify no dropping defender is occupying the hot window.')],
[Q('Which is a sound pre-snap read?', ['Form a hypothesis and plan post-snap confirmation','Assume two-high must stay Cover 2','Choose a target solely from jersey color'],0,'Pre-snap evidence must be confirmed after the snap.'),Q('MOFC usually means?', ['A deep middle field defender closes the post','There are always two deep safeties','The defense has no linebackers'],0,'MOFC is a structural label for middle-of-field closed coverage.'),Q('MOFO usually describes?', ['An open deep middle between split safeties','A center snap count','A specific offensive run gap'],0,'MOFO often points to split deep safety structures.'),Q('The apex widens with the flat. What is the stick QB often reading?', ['An inside window that widener left','A punt landing spot','The center’s cleats'],0,'The flat movement can open space for the stick route.'),Q('A primary corner route is capped by deep help. What is good QB process?', ['Progress to another defined answer','Force the corner throw regardless','Stop reading defenders'],0,'Capped routes require moving through the progression.'),Q('What is a hot answer?', ['A timed quick response to an unblocked pressure threat','A touchdown on every down','Only a deep post route'],0,'Hot options are coordinated responses to protection shortages.'),Q('Why is a receiver who looks open pre-snap not guaranteed open?', ['Coverage rotation and match rules develop after snap','Offense cannot pass in second quarter','The ball changes shape'],0,'The post-snap defense controls the actual throwing window.'),Q('On a high-low read, what should be watched?', ['The conflict defender’s depth and response','Only QB jersey number','Who calls the coin flip'],0,'Defender movement selects which route level becomes available.'),Q('What should the QB do after identifying six potential rushers versus five protectors?', ['Confirm pressure response and available quick lane','Ignore rush and wait for deep routes','Ask the defense to pause'],0,'Protection and immediate coverage determine the answer.'),Q('A disguised defender drops into a hot lane. What is the right principle?', ['Confirm the replacement before throwing','Always throw hot without looking','Never use short passes'],0,'Replacement defenders can trap the automatic hot throw.')]),
L(17,'Defensive Checks vs Trips, Bunch & Motion','Learn how high-level defenses adjust to overloads and routes without losing run-fit integrity.',
['Identify strength and receiver numbering','Understand trips and bunch adjustment families','Coordinate pressure, coverage and run fit'],[
C('Trips stresses distribution','Trips describes three eligible receiving threats to one side, often labeled No. 1 outside to No. 3 inside. Defenses must assign routes without leaving the opposite side or run fit unattended.','Against 3x1, a defense might poach a backside safety to help handle No. 3 vertical, depending on its rules.','High-level defense starts by matching offensive threats to coverage players, not by mechanically assigning a defender to each colored circle.','Count No. 1, No. 2, No. 3 from outside in, then identify who could carry No. 3 vertical.'),
C('Bunch and stack change leverage','Tight bunch and stack alignments help offenses create releases and rubs that complicate man coverage. Defenses may use switch, box or banjo-type communication to distribute crossing routes rather than trail blindly.','Three receivers release from a tight bunch; defenders call a switch so the first outside release belongs to the outside defender.','A coverage that wins against a spread alignment can fail without an agreed bunch check. Alignments change the assignment rules before the ball moves.','Track receiver releases, not just starting positions. Which defender takes which exit route?'),
C('Motion as information and stress','Motion can change strength, create new run-surface numbers, move a receiver across the formation or expose a defensive coverage rule. The defensive response must preserve gap fit and matching assignments.','A slot jets across the formation; the secondary rotates responsibilities rather than a single corner following the motion across.','A defender tracking motion is a clue, not proof of man coverage. The true test is what assignments the defense takes when the receivers release at the snap.','Before and after motion, recount eligible threats and which defender has force to the newly strong side.'),
C('Match rules are conditional','In match coverage, defenders react to route distribution: No. 2 vertical, No. 2 out and fast No. 3 can trigger different switches. Different teams use distinct calls, so principles matter more than memorizing one staff’s vocabulary.','With No. 2 out and No. 1 vertical, some Palms variants allow the corner to trigger on No. 2 while the safety handles No. 1.','Pattern matching combines the spacing awareness of zone with man-like responses to routes; that is why a post-snap freeze frame can be misleading.','Compare the safety and corner response when No. 2 runs upfield versus breaking immediately out.'),
C('Run fits survive pass checks','When a defense widens a linebacker or rotates a safety to handle three receivers, another defender may need to replace the missing run-fit player. Checks must protect both the run box and coverage distribution.','A nickel walks out over a slot and the safety inserts downhill, preserving the alley fit against a run to the trips side.','Football is coordinated: removing a coverage weakness can create a run weakness if the defensive front and second level do not communicate.','After a motion or trips check, identify every gap defender and the new force/alley support.')],
[Q('How are No. 1–3 receivers commonly numbered on a trips side?', ['Outside inward','From the center outward only','Alphabetically by name'],0,'Many systems number eligible receivers outside in.'),Q('What makes trips challenging?', ['Three route threats on one side need coordinated distribution','The offense has twelve players','The QB must punt'],0,'Trips overloads one side’s coverage rules.'),Q('What can a poach check address in a 3x1 structure?', ['No. 3 vertical from trips','Only a field goal snap','An illegal center'],0,'Some poach principles use a backside safety to handle No. 3 vertical.'),Q('Against a tight bunch, what can a switch/banjo call help?', ['Exchanging receivers by release','Changing field length','Assigning three ball carriers'],0,'Defenders exchange crossing routes instead of chasing through traffic.'),Q('A corner follows motion before snap. Which conclusion is safest?', ['It is a clue, not a guarantee of man','It proves man coverage','It is automatically offsides'],0,'Coverage can exchange assignments after motion.'),Q('What can change when a nickel widens to the slot?', ['The run fit needs replacement support','The offense gains a first down','The play clock is reset'],0,'Defensive adjustments must account for displaced run defenders.'),Q('Why are match-coverage labels not universal?', ['Teams use different distribution rules and terminology','Every team has identical play calls','A quarterback selects the defense'],0,'Match families depend on a staff’s defined rules.'),Q('What helps a defender cover fast crossing routes from a bunch?', ['A prearranged exchange rule','Standing in the same place always','Abandoning every assignment'],0,'Coverage checks tell defenders who takes each release.'),Q('Which observation best reveals motion’s effect?', ['How defensive matchups and fit change after motion','Only where the QB stands','The scoreboard font'],0,'Motion can transform coverage strength and run force responsibilities.'),Q('If No. 2 immediately releases out in some Palms calls, what may happen?', ['Corner triggers on No. 2 and safety takes No. 1','The QB automatically throws long','All defenders rush'],0,'A two-read/Palm family can switch assignments based on No. 2 release.')]),
L(18,'Complementary Calls & Offensive Sequencing','Design related plays that share a believable presentation but attack different defensive answers.',
['Explain run-pass complementarity','Build a three-call constraint sequence','Recognize when a counter stops being effective'],[
C('Constraint plays punish defensive habits','An offense wants its primary run or pass to succeed, then uses constraints when defenders begin cheating toward stopping it. Constraints may include boot, split-zone action, bubbles or counters.','After defenders squeeze hard on an inside-zone look, a bootleg off similar backfield action can stress the edge and flat coverage.','The complementary play needs believable shared cues; simply calling a random new play is not the same as sequencing. Watch which defender is consistently making a particular choice.','Compare the first two steps of base play and complement. Which defensive reaction should the second call punish?'),
C('One formation, multiple answers','Packaging several concepts from the same personnel grouping and formation reduces the defense’s ability to predict intent. The offense can shift from an inside run to a quick pass or play-action without a wholesale substitution.','From 11 personnel Trips Right, the coordinator calls inside zone, a tagged bubble, then a Flood shot off a run presentation.','Common structure matters more than endless playbook size. High-performing offenses build families of calls whose assignments and language reinforce each other.','When two plays look identical before the snap, identify the one post-snap defensive cue that distinguishes their purpose.'),
C('Coverage beaters are conditional','A Smash concept can work well against some corner/flat relationships; Mesh pressures man and certain match rules; Flood can stretch an outside zone. None is a universal answer to every defense.','Calling Mesh into a defense that passes crossers effectively may leave fewer easy windows than anticipated.','Sound sequencing carries multiple counters, not magic “beat Cover 3” buttons. Match the concept to the actual defenders and protection picture.','After a concept succeeds, ask which defensive adjustment could erase that throwing window.'),
C('Build a three-play answer tree','An answer tree begins with a base call, anticipates how defenders could overplay it, and lists a complement for each defensive response. A third answer protects against the defense’s counter-adjustment.','Base: inside zone. If the apex expands for a bubble fake, keep the inside run; if the apex squeezes run, throw the available perimeter option within the play’s rules.','Play sequencing should respond to observable behavior, not superstitions like “run, run, pass.” A successful play can be repeated if the defense still gives it away.','Write two alternative defender reactions and the play element that targets each one.'),
C('Down, distance and clock veto pretty plays','A deep shot might be strategically poor on third-and-2 when the offense needs a first down, and a slow counter may be unsuited to a hurry-up situation. A coherent coordinator balances matchup and game state.','Up by four with 1:20 left and the opponent short on timeouts, a high-risk long pass may unnecessarily stop the clock.','Smart play calling weighs conversion chance, time, field position, turnover cost and opponent tendencies; it does not simply reward the fanciest diagram.','Before evaluating a call, read down, distance, score and clock without looking at the play art.')],
[Q('What defines a true constraint play?', ['It punishes the defense’s reaction to a related base play','It is randomly unrelated to the previous call','It must be a field goal'],0,'Constraints counter defensive habits built around the base action.'),Q('Why use the same formation for multiple concepts?', ['Disguise intent and reuse assignment families','Guarantee the same yards every time','Prevent defensive substitutions forever'],0,'A common look can present different post-snap threats.'),Q('A defender consistently squeezes to stop zone. Which complement might stress their reaction?', ['Bootleg off similar run action','Repeat only long punts','Abandon all play-action'],0,'Boot can punish aggressive run-keying.'),Q('Why is “Mesh beats man” not universally reliable?', ['Coverage exchanges and rush timing can change the result','Mesh is always illegal','Man coverage has no defenders'],0,'Defensive checks and pressure can negate expected advantages.'),Q('What is an answer tree?', ['Base play and planned responses to defensive reactions','One formation run forever','A list of final scores'],0,'Coordinators plan contingencies against observable adjustments.'),Q('Should a successful play always be abandoned for variety?', ['No, repeat it if matchup still favors it','Yes, repeating is illegal','Only after a field goal'],0,'A good call can be reused when the defensive response remains favorable.'),Q('A two-play sequence looks identical pre-snap. What is its advantage?', ['Defenders cannot rely only on alignment to identify intent','The QB gets two forward passes','The offense automatically draws a flag'],0,'Similar presentations increase the cost of guessing.'),Q('Why must down-and-distance be considered?', ['A beautiful call may not meet the needed objective','It changes which team has eleven players','It forces all teams into Nickel'],0,'A call needs to serve the game situation.'),Q('What makes a third complementary call useful?', ['It answers the defender adjustment that countered the second','It always throws to the same player','It eliminates pass protection'],0,'Third calls can punish the defense for adapting to the first counter.'),Q('What is the most useful trigger for changing a call?', ['A repeated identifiable defensive reaction','A random number unrelated to football','A fixed rule to alternate run/pass'],0,'Play selection is driven by opponent tendencies and current context.')]),
L(19,'Spread Offense, RPOs & Option Reads','Separate pre-snap access throws, post-snap conflict reads and true run options.',
['Distinguish RPO from option run','Identify conflict defenders and legal constraints','Evaluate post-snap read decisions'],[
C('Spread spacing changes defender math','Spread formations widen eligible threats and ask defenders to cover width while maintaining interior run fits. Defenses counter with apex alignments, safety inserts and match coverages.','A 2x2 spread alignment widens two apex defenders, potentially leaving a lighter interior box than a condensed formation.','Spacing itself is not a strategy; the offense must know whether the defense responds by widening personnel, rotating a safety or keeping the box heavy.','Count box and overhang defenders before and after the offense spreads its formation.'),
C('Pre-snap access throws','An access throw or tagged perimeter pass may be available when a receiver has a favorable cushion or numbers before the snap. A pre-snap “gift” decision is not necessarily a post-snap RPO read.','A corner gives a wide receiver ample cushion and the quarterback checks to an immediate hitch as an access answer.','Avoid calling every perimeter pass an RPO. The read can be made before the snap and the play need not involve a handoff fake.','Ask whether the QB made the decision before the snap or read a defender while the mesh developed.'),
C('The true run-pass conflict','A run-pass option pairs a run with a pass element and reads a designated defender during the mesh or immediate post-snap phase. If the defender inserts against the run, a passing window may appear; if they stay wide, the run may keep its numbers.','Inside zone is paired with a glance behind an apex/linebacker: the QB watches that defender before handing off or delivering the quick glance within legal timing.','The defender must actually have two plausible responsibilities. An RPO is a timed decision with blocking and eligibility restrictions, not permission to wait indefinitely and then throw.','Freeze the QB at the mesh and name the conflict defender, both choices, and the window that closes first.'),
C('Zone read is an option run','Zone read often leaves an edge defender unblocked and reads their movement: give if they stay to contain the QB, keep if they crash toward the running back, subject to team rules. It is a run-read option, not automatically a pass option.','On zone read, an edge defender crashes on the back; the QB may keep around the edge if no scrape-exchange defender replaces the contain assignment.','The defensive scrape exchange shows why “always keep if end crashes” can be wrong: a linebacker may replace the edge defender’s responsibility.','Track both the unblocked edge defender and the next defender who can scrape over the top.'),
C('Eligibility and downfield constraints','On an RPO with a forward pass, offensive linemen cannot drift too far illegally downfield before the ball is thrown. Exact thresholds and enforcement differ by NCAA, high school and NFL rulebooks; timing and technique matter.','If linemen drive downfield on an inside-zone look while the QB delays the throw, the team may risk an ineligible-player-downfield foul.','The technical legal constraint is part of play design. College-level understanding means identifying not only the apparent open throw but whether the timing and protection make it legitimate.','On slow-developing RPOs, check how far linemen have progressed when the quarterback releases the pass.')],
[Q('What does spread spacing challenge?', ['Defensive width and box fits at the same time','Only the kickoff tee','The number of referees'],0,'Widened receivers stress horizontal and interior responsibilities.'),Q('How is a pre-snap access throw different from post-snap RPO?', ['Decision can be made before snap without a mesh read','It requires an unblocked end always','It is always a punt'],0,'Access answers are often determined by pre-snap spacing.'),Q('An RPO conflict defender steps downhill toward the run. What may open?', ['The pass window behind or outside that defender','An automatic extra offensive player','A forced touchdown'],0,'Inserting against the run can vacate a passing window.'),Q('On zone read, an unblocked end crashes toward the back. What could the QB do?', ['Keep if contain is not replaced','Always throw a deep pass','Never read the end'],0,'Zone read often uses the edge defender to decide give or keep.'),Q('Why must a QB also watch a scraping linebacker?', ['They can replace the crashing edge as contain','They are not allowed to tackle','They change the clock by moving'],0,'Scrape exchange can trap a simple give/keep read.'),Q('What is a late RPO pass’s possible penalty risk?', ['Ineligible linemen illegally downfield','Delay of game on every completion','A false start after the snap'],0,'RPO timing must respect downfield eligibility rules.'),Q('Is every pass next to an inside-zone fake an RPO?', ['No, exact decision structure matters','Yes, always','Only in fourth quarter'],0,'A pre-called play-action pass is not necessarily an RPO read.'),Q('What makes a post-snap RPO defender a useful key?', ['They must choose between competing run and pass responsibilities','They wear a particular jersey number','They can never be blocked'],0,'RPOs exploit a two-way conflict in one defender.'),Q('If the apex stays wide on the bubble threat, what may be favorable?', ['An interior run if box count and blocks support it','Throw directly to the covered bubble regardless','End the play without snap'],0,'Defender width may leave room for the run.'),Q('Why does the NCAA-vs-NFL rulebook matter for RPO coaching?', ['Pass eligibility and downfield constraints can differ','RPOs are not used in college','Defenders have a different number of arms'],0,'Legal timing depends on the governing rules.')]),
L(20,'College Game Planning & Full-Play Diagnosis','Finish by combining offensive structure, defensive checks, protection, reads, and situation into one complete call.',
['Build an opponent tendency report','Diagnose all eleven offensive assignments','Explain a defense’s counter and the coordinator’s next answer'],[
C('Start with self-scout and opponent scouting','High-performing college teams analyze their own tells and opponents’ tendencies by personnel, formation, down, distance, field zone and game context. A tendency informs a decision but is never a certainty.','The defense runs a six-man box against 11 personnel on first down, but rotates to single-high coverage in red-zone third-and-medium.','A scouting report is only useful when tied to the next actionable decision. Track evidence sample sizes and do not invent certainty from two observed snaps.','Look for repeated patterns under comparable conditions, rather than combining unrelated third-down and goal-line plays.'),
C('Define an entire offensive play call','A college play call specifies personnel, formation, motion or shift if present, run/pass concept, line protection or blocking scheme, receiver assignments, back responsibility, QB read and alerts. Team terminology differs.','Illustrative call: 11 personnel, Gun Trips Right, Inside Zone with a tagged glance read. Five linemen execute zone rules, the RB meshes on zone track, eligible receivers run their tagged assignments, QB reads the defined conflict defender.','An arrow chart for two receivers is not a complete play. Every one of the eleven offensive players contributes to the scheme, even if the ball goes elsewhere.','Pause the alignment and assign a purpose to QB, RB, five linemen, three WRs and TE before judging success.'),
C('Read the opponent’s defensive check','After the offense declares its formation and motion, the defense may change front, shift the force player, rotate safety support or check coverage. A meaningful diagnosis compares the new run fit and pass distribution.','Trips motion pulls a nickel wider, so the defense inserts a safety to maintain the box while the backside safety gains responsibility for No. 3 vertical.','Defensive adjustment has a cost. Identify what improved and what may have become newly vulnerable rather than saying “the defense adjusted.”','Identify each moved defender’s new job and who replaced their old responsibility.'),
C('Make the quarterback read with protection','Before snap: identify shell, box, pressure threats and likely conflict. After snap: verify post safety, rush and key defender; make the assigned throw or handoff within timing and eligibility rules.','Two-high looks stay split, apex squeezes against inside zone, protection accounts for the rush, so the quick tagged throw may be available. If the apex stays wide, the inside run may be preferred.','The answer depends on a sequence of observations, not guessing a famous play call. QB decision-making is constrained by the legal and mechanical design of the play.','Narrate “I expected... then I saw... so I decided...” and name the single clue that would make you change the answer.'),
C('Post-play diagnosis and next adjustment','Separate call quality, read quality and execution. Judge a successful play that was strategically poor differently from a smart decision derailed by a dropped ball or a defender making an outstanding play. Then propose the next complementary call.','The QB correctly throws hot versus an unblocked rusher, but a defender makes a difficult interception. Review the read, throwing lane, replacement coverage and protection before declaring the original call bad.','The highest-level learning outcome is identifying what the opponent did and prescribing a coherent next answer without assuming an outcome proves the decision was right or wrong.','Write a four-sentence review: defensive structure, offensive intent, decisive read, and next counter.')],
[Q('What is a meaningful self-scout category?', ['Formation tendency on third-and-medium','Preferred stadium music','Uniform color at halftime'],0,'Self-scout identifies recurring plays in comparable situations.'),Q('Why is a tendency not a guarantee?', ['Defenses disguise and adapt, and sample sizes vary','Football bans tendencies','Every team uses exactly one play'],0,'Frequency guides probability, not certainty.'),Q('Which list best describes a complete offensive call?', ['Personnel, formation, assignments, protection, QB read and alerts','Primary WR arrow and the final score','Only a run direction'],0,'A full play defines eleven roles and how the decision is made.'),Q('Why can defense adjust to trips without adding a player?', ['Redistribute coverage and run-fit responsibilities','Teams may play twelve defenders','The football shrinks'],0,'Defensive checks redistribute existing responsibilities.'),Q('What should be confirmed after a two-high shell?', ['Actual post-snap safeties, rush and coverage rules','Only the weather','The length of the stadium'],0,'Actual coverage and rush may differ from the pre-snap appearance.'),Q('Which QB narration reflects a real read?', ['“I expected two high, apex squeezed, I threw the defined quick answer”','“I chose the tallest receiver no matter the coverage”','“I never checked the rush”'],0,'A real read identifies hypothesis, defender action and resulting answer.'),Q('A receiver drops a well-read pass. What should be evaluated?', ['Decision quality separately from execution','The play call must have been wrong','The defense was automatically illegal'],0,'Outcome alone does not prove decision quality.'),Q('A blitzing linebacker is replaced by a dropping end. What must the QB check?', ['Whether the replacement covers the hot lane','Whether the center wore gloves','Whether the down number changed'],0,'Replacement coverage can close the presumed passing window.'),Q('Why should a complete play name the backside receiver’s route?', ['They may clear help, occupy coverage or be a secondary answer','Only targeted players matter','They never move on a passing play'],0,'Every eligible player’s assignment supports the concept.'),Q('What belongs in a four-sentence game-plan review?', ['Structure, intent, decisive read, next counter','Score, mascot, song, crowd','Only who won the play'],0,'A complete diagnosis links opponent behavior to decision-making and the next adjustment.')])
];
